] , and MetaRAG [
[58](#bib.bib12)
] embed decisions about when to query external knowledge, how to structure intermediate reasoning, and when to self-critique directly into a single LLM, enabling adaptive retrieval and reflection within one policy. Plan- and graph-centric frameworks like PAR-RAG [
[54](#bib.bib11)
] and GraphRAG-R1 [
[51](#bib.bib10)
] further externalize this agency into explicit multi-step plans or reasoning graphs that guide tool calls and evidence aggregation for multi-hop questions. In contrast, multi-agent systems such as MA-RAG [
[29](#bib.bib8)
] , MAO-ARAG [
[8](#bib.bib9)
] and SIRAG [
