3 Preliminaries
3.2 Threat Model: Corpus Knowledge Poisoning
We define three attack strategies: Random , Near , and Far . Each strategy specifies how the attacker selects (or generates) the N N adversarial documents. Under the
*Random*
strategy, the attacker uniformly samples N N documents from a pool of candidate adversarial documents, which was created using some approach (e.g., by prompting an LLM). Under the
*Near*
(
*Far*
) strategy, the attacker selects the N N documents from the pool that are closest (farthest) to the centroid of the benign retrieved documents in an embedding space. Each strategy corresponds to a plausible real-world approach an attacker might adopt when performing an attack. The Near and Far strategies can be applied, for example, by observing attribution outcomes in a RAG system (
[22](#bib.bib25)
) . That is, it is often the case that the documents shown by a RAG system to provide support for the generated response are those in D D . To the best of our knowledge, the Near and Far strategies are novel to our study. We show in Section
[6.2](#S6.SS2)
that the type of attack has an impact on the attack effectiveness.", "Privacy-Preserving RAG by Concealing Sensitive  Information from External LLMs Journal: Knowledge-Based Systems
