2 Related Work
Retrieval-Augmented Generation. LLMs require external knowledge to alleviate the factuality drawbacks. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) has been regarded as an effective solution to mitigate the aforementioned hallucinations and temporal misalignment issues inherent in large language models, especially for knowledge-intensive tasks. Generally, studies of RAG can be categorized into three types (
[Gao et al. 2023](#bib.bib13)
) , namely Naive RAG, Advanced RAG, and Modular RAG. Naive RAG means a straightforward Retrieve-then-Read framework (
[Lewis et al. 2020](#bib.bib22)
;
[Karpukhin et al. 2020](#bib.bib20)
;
[Izacard et al. 2022](#bib.bib16)
) . To enhance retrieval quality, the Advanced RAG builds upon the foundation of Naive RAG by incorporating pre-retrieval (
[Li et al. 2023a](#bib.bib23)
) and post-retrieval (
[Jiang et al. 2023](#bib.bib18)
;
[Xu et al. 2023](#bib.bib37)
) strategies. Modular RAG improves the overall performance by decomposing the Retrieve-then-Read framework into fine-grained modules with distinct functionalities, such as a search module (
[Wang et al. 2023](#bib.bib35)
) , memory module (
[Cheng et al. 2024](#bib.bib7)
) .", "Agentic AI and Retrieval-Augmented Models in Straight-Through Underwriting
